# Code Review Report

**Course:** SVV Lab Task - Static Verification of C++ Code
**Program reviewed:** `game.cpp` (a small text-based monster battle game)
**Method:** Manual static code review by defect category (Initialization, Pointers, Arrays/buffers, Loops, Arithmetic, Control flow, Function call, Classes), checked with `g++ -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion`.
**Note:** Line numbers refer to `game.cpp` with one statement per line.

**Summary:** 22 defects found: 14 High, 5 Medium, 3 Low.

| Line number | Defect type | Why it is a problem | Proposed solution | Severity |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 10-22 | Classes | `Monster` fields are public and the constructor assigns members in its body instead of the initializer list. | Make fields private with accessors and use an initializer list. | Low |
| 17, 40 | Function call | `std::string` is passed by value in the `Monster` constructor and `spawnMonster`, causing unnecessary copies. | Use `const std::string&`. | Low |
| 32 | Initialization | The `monsters[]` array is never initialized, so its slots hold garbage pointers. | Initialize with `monsters[MAX_MONSTERS] = {nullptr}` or a loop that sets each slot to `nullptr`. | Low |
| 41 | Arrays/buffers | `spawnMonster` has no bounds check. `main` spawns 6 monsters (line 119) and `readBoss` spawns a 7th, but the array holds only 5, so it writes out of bounds. | Check `monsterCount < MAX_MONSTERS` before inserting and return false on failure. Better: use `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Monster>>`. | High |
| 47 | Control flow | `=` is used instead of `==`. This overwrites every monster's id and is true for any non-zero id, so the first monster is always returned. | Change to `if (monsters[i]->id == id)`. | High |
| 54, 56 | Pointers | `findMonster` can return `nullptr` (e.g. id 99 at line 121), but `m->health` is used without a null check. | Add `if (m == nullptr) return -1;` (or throw an exception). | High |
| 55, 56 | Arithmetic | `unsigned int dmg = damage` is a signed-to-unsigned conversion and `m->health - dmg` is unsigned. `< 0` is always false, so the guard never works, and a negative damage becomes a huge number. | Keep `damage` as `int`, reject `damage < 0`, and test `if (m->health < damage)`. | High |
| 58 | Arithmetic | Because the check on line 56 never triggers, health can go negative. | Clamp: `m->health = std::max(0, m->health - damage);` | Medium |
| 64 | Loops | The loop uses `i <= monsterCount` (off by one). It reads `monsters[monsterCount]`, an uninitialized slot, and dereferences it. | Change to `i < monsterCount`. | High |
| 70-73 | Function call | `getMonsterLabel` returns a reference (`std::string&`) to the local variable `label`, which is destroyed on return, so the reference dangles. Line 72 also dereferences `m` without a null check. | Return `std::string` by value and add a null check. | High |
| 77-79 | Initialization | `double sum;` is uninitialized, so `+=` adds to garbage. | Write `double sum = 0.0;` | High |
| 80 | Arithmetic | If `monsterCount == 0` this divides by zero. It also silently converts `double` to `int`. | Add `if (monsterCount == 0) return 0;` and use `static_cast<int>(...)`, or return `double`. | Medium |
| 84-86 | Pointers | `m` is not checked for null. After `delete m` the code still reads `m->name` (use-after-free). | Print the name before deleting and check `m != nullptr`. | High |
| 85 | Classes | The deleted pointer stays in `monsters[]` and `monsterCount` is not reduced. The destructor will delete it again (double free) and later lookups use a dangling pointer. | Remove the entry (shift the array down) and decrement `monsterCount`, or set the slot to `nullptr`. | High |
| 91 | Arithmetic | `attack * armorPercent / 100` is integer division, so the fraction is lost before conversion to `double`. It can also overflow `int`. The formula also adds armor to the damage rather than reducing it. | Use `attack * (100 - armorPercent) / 100.0`. | Medium |
| 94-98 | Control flow | `playerRank` has no `return` when `score <= 100`, which is undefined behavior. `playerRank(50)` on line 126 hits this case. | Add a final `return 3;` (or `else return 3;`). | High |
| 102, 105 | Arrays/buffers | `std::cin >> name` into a 10-char buffer has no length limit, so a name of 10 or more characters overflows it. | Use `std::string name; std::cin >> name;` | High |
| 103, 106 | Initialization | `health` is uninitialized. If `cin` fails, garbage is passed to `spawnMonster`. | Write `int health = 0;` and check `if (!(std::cin >> health))`. | Medium |
| 109 | Pointers | `delete name` is used on memory from `new char[10]`. This `new[]` / `delete` mismatch is undefined behavior. | Use `delete[] name`, or avoid it by using `std::string`. | High |
| 119 | Arrays/buffers | The 6th `spawnMonster` call exceeds `MAX_MONSTERS = 5`. | Raise the limit or handle the failure returned by the bounds check at line 41. | High |
| 120 | Pointers | `bonus` is allocated with `new` and never freed (memory leak). It is also unused. | Remove it, or `delete bonus;` (or use a smart pointer). | Medium |
| 129 | Classes | `Game copy = game;` makes a shallow copy of the raw pointer array. `Game` has no copy constructor or copy assignment operator (Rule of Three), so both objects delete the same monsters (double free). | Delete the copy operations (`Game(const Game&) = delete;`), write deep-copy ones, or use `unique_ptr`. | High |
